if i want to tweak the 50% swipe trigger , what code do i change
In app.js, the initGestures call at the bottom of the file has these two lines:


commitThresholdH: () => window.innerWidth  * 0.5,
commitThresholdV: () => window.innerHeight * 0.5,
Change 0.5 to whatever fraction you want — e.g. 0.35 for 35% of screen width/height. H is the left/right (week) swipe, V is the up/down (month) swipe.

For the expanded day view, the threshold is separately in app.js inside _handleExpEnd:


if (Math.abs(dx) >= window.innerWidth * 0.5) {
Same idea — change 0.5 there to adjust the day-swipe trigger independently.


1. I’m building a mobile calendar UI and running into gesture‑conflict issues. Editable text areas (notes, diary entries, event descriptions) need vertical scrolling, but the page also supports swipe navigation for changing days, weeks, and months. On Android WebView, these gestures interfere with each other — scrolling inside a text field often triggers navigation.

Please propose a solution using dedicated swipe areas, based on this layout: I've included images of how it might be achieved

The top and bottom edges of the screen act as swipe zones for left/right navigation (e.g., change week or month).

A central vertical strip acts as the swipe zone for up/down navigation (e.g., change day).

Swipes should only trigger navigation when the gesture begins inside these zones.

Interactions inside editable text areas must never trigger navigation.

Each swipe zone should provide subtle but clear visual feedback, such as a soft glow, when touched.

The glow should make the purpose of the zone obvious without being visually intrusive.

The goal is to eliminate gesture conflicts while keeping navigation intuitive and mobile‑friendly.

